  <section id="zones" aria-labelledby="zones-h">
    <h2 id="zones-h">The Five Heart Rate Zones</h2>
    <figure class="tp-fig"><img src="/assets/images/heart-rate-zones-chart.webp" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Heart rate zones 1 to 5 for a 40-year-old with max heart rate 180 and resting heart rate 60"><figcaption>Karvonen heart rate zones for a 40-year-old.</figcaption></figure><div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table"><caption>Heart rate training zones</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Zone</th><th scope="col">Intensity</th><th scope="col">What it trains</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">1 &middot; Very light</th><td>50&ndash;60%</td><td>Recovery, warm-up</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">2 &middot; Light</th><td>60&ndash;70%</td><td>Aerobic base, fat oxidation, endurance</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">3 &middot; Moderate</th><td>70&ndash;80%</td><td>Aerobic capacity, tempo</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">4 &middot; Hard</th><td>80&ndash;90%</td><td>Lactate threshold</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">5 &middot; Maximum</th><td>90&ndash;100%</td><td>VO2 max, speed, short intervals</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  </section>

  <section id="karvonen" aria-labelledby="karvonen-h">
    <h2 id="karvonen-h">The Karvonen Formula</h2>
    <p class="tp-note" style="font-size:17px!important"><span class="tp-kbd">Target HR = resting HR + (max HR &minus; resting HR) &times; intensity</span></p><p>Example: max 185, resting 60, zone 2 at 60&ndash;70%: 60 + 125 &times; 0.6 = 135 to 60 + 125 &times; 0.7 = 148 bpm. Because it accounts for your resting heart rate, Karvonen zones fit fit people better than plain percentages of maximum.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="zone2" aria-labelledby="zone2-h">
    <h2 id="zone2-h">Zone 2 Training</h2>
    <p>Zone 2 is easy enough to hold a conversation and to sustain for an hour or more. Most endurance coaches put around three quarters of weekly training here, because it builds the heart&rsquo;s stroke volume, capillaries and mitochondria with little fatigue. If you cannot speak in full sentences, slow down. Track your aerobic fitness over time with the <a href="/vo2-max-calculator/">VO2 max calculator</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="use" aria-labelledby="use-h">
    <h2 id="use-h">How to Train With Heart Rate Zones</h2>
    <p>A simple week for general fitness: three or four sessions mostly in zones 2&ndash;3, plus one session with short intervals in zones 4&ndash;5. Wrist heart rate monitors lag and can misread during intervals; chest straps are more accurate. Heat, caffeine, stress and poor sleep all raise heart rate, so judge by feel on those days. For running speeds that match your zones, see the <a href="/running-pace-calculator/">running pace calculator</a>.</p>
  </section>

